<title>Video: Converting between Scientific Notation and Decimal Form</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about rewriting scientific notation in decimal form and decimal form in
  scientific notation.</para>
<para>To support understanding of meaning, main points, and important details, invite English language students or students with disabilities to take notes from a teacher demonstration or while watching a worked solutions video. This helps to ensure that they identify implicit ideas in increasingly complex ways. </para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <div class="os-raise-video-container"><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/65d24174dc794f36c8a7e4f9f62645f31aea59f8">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/55eb879a1a8a3889b65fdce986f2a629ba0c60e2" srclang="en_us">
      https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/65d24174dc794f36c8a7e4f9f62645f31aea59f8
    </track></source></video></div>
</div>


<title>5.6.4: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
    understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which of the following equations is true?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(7.64\times 10^3 = 76,400\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Multiplying by \(10^3\) means multiplying by 1000.
        \(7.64\times 1000 = 7,640\). The answer is \(8.5\times 10^{-4} = 0.00085\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(9.11\times 10^5 = 91,100\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Multiplying by \(10^5\) means multiplying by 100,000.
        \(9.11\times 100,000 = 911,000\). The answer is \(8.5\times 10^{-4} = 0.00085\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(8.5\times 10^{-4} = 0.00085\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: Multiplying by \(10^{-4}\) means dividing by 10,000. The values are
        equivalent.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(2.36\times 10^{-5} = 0.00000236\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Multiplying by \(10^{-5}\) means dividing by 100,000.
        \(2.36\times 10^{-5} = 0.0000236\). The answer is \(8.5\times 10^{-4} = 0.00085\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title> 5.6.4: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on
    their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish
    to share it with those who would benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Using Scientific Notation</title>
<para>Working with very large or very small numbers can be awkward. Since our number system is base ten, we can use powers
  of ten to rewrite very large or very small numbers to make them easier to work with. Consider the numbers 4,000 and
  0.004.</para>
<para>Using place value, we can rewrite the numbers 4,000 and 0.004. We know that 4,000 means \(4\times 1,000\) and 0.004
  means \(4\times\frac1{1000}\).</para>
<para>If we write the 1,000 as a power of ten in exponential form, we can rewrite these numbers in this way:</para>

<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-evenly">
  <para>4,000 </para>
  <para>\(4\times 1,000\)</para>
  <para>\(4\times 10^3\) </para>
</div>


<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-evenly">
  <para> 0.004 </para>
  <para> \(4\times\frac1{1000}\) </para>
  <para> \(4\times\frac1{10^3}\) </para>
  <para> \(4\times 10^{-3}\) </para>
</div>

<para>When a number is written as a product of two numbers, where the first factor is a number greater than or equal to 1
  but less than 10, and the second factor is a power of 10 written in exponential form, it is said to be in scientific
  notation.</para>
<para>A number is expressed in scientific notation when it is of what form?</para>
<para>\(a\times 10^n\) where \(1 \leq&amp;boxv;a&amp;boxv;&lt; 10\) and \(n\) is an integer.</para>
<para>It is customary in scientific notation to use × as the multiplication sign, even though we avoid using this
  sign elsewhere in algebra.</para>

<div>
  <title>To convert a decimal to scientific notation:</title>
  <para>Step 1. Move the decimal point so that the first factor is greater than or equal to 1 but less than 10.</para>
  <para>Step 2. Count the number of decimal places, \(n\), that the decimal point was moved.</para>
  <para>Step 3. Write the number as a product with a power of 10. If the original number is</para>
  <list>
    <item> greater than 1, the power of 10 will be \(10^n\). </item>
    <item> between 0 and 1, the power of 10 will be \(10^{−n}\). </item>
  </list>
  <para>Step 4. Check.</para>
</div>

<para>Example: Write in scientific notation:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> 37,000 
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/43b4a38b938bd6ff8b0db5bf19762bba59c4cef5"/></media></figure></item>
  <item> 0.0052 
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1daab4b538a14fc662e6cf03b3a415755bdb69be"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<title> To convert scientific notation to decimal form:</title>
<para>Step 1. Determine the exponent, \(n\), on the factor of 10.</para>
<para>Step 2. Move the decimal \(n\) places, adding zeros if needed.</para>
<list>
  <item> If the exponent is positive, move the decimal point \(n\) places to the right. </item>
  <item> If the exponent is negative, move the decimal point \(n\) places to the left. </item>
</list>
<para>Step 3. Check.</para>
<para>Example: Write in decimal form:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(6.2 \times 10^3\) 
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6015238e3522b3a2162047fae197819645a94470"/></media></figure></item>
  
  <item> \(-8.9\times10^{-2}\) 
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a4f3a281bc009fcf08f2c70fee42aa2dfcd9cddc"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<title>Try It: Using Scientific Notation</title>
<para>Write in scientific notation.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> 65,000,000 </item>
  <item> 0.00034 </item>
</list>
<para>Write in decimal form.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(5.3 \times10^5
    \)</item>
  <item> \(4.25 \times10^{-3}\)</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to write the numbers:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(
    6.5 \times10^7
    \)</item>
  <item>\(
    3.4\times10^{-4}
    \)</item>
  <item> 530,000 </item>
  <item> 0.00425 </item>
</list></section>
